== 4. Counterfeit Graces

#strong[The fourth #emph[device] that Satan has to keep the soul in a sad, doubting, and questioning condition, is,]

BY suggesting to them that their graces are not true, but counterfeit. “All is not gold that glitters,” says Satan; “all is not grace that you count grace, that you call grace. That which#footnote[Yet it must be granted that many a fair flower may grow out of a stinking root, and many sweet dispositions and fair actions may be where there is only the corrupt root of nature.] you call faith is only a fancy, and that which you call zeal is only unnatural heat and passion. And that light you have is only common; it falls short of what many have attained who are now in hell,” etc. Satan does not labor more mightily to persuade hypocrites that their graces are true, when they are counterfeit, than he does to persuade precious souls that their graces are counterfeit, when indeed they are true, and such as will bear the touchstone of Christ, etc.

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is seriously to consider that grace is taken in two ways.

First, it is taken for the gracious goodwill and favor of God, by which he is pleased, of his own free love, to accept some in Christ as his own. Some call this the first grace, because it is the fountain of all other graces, and the spring from which they flow. And it is called grace because it makes a man gracious with God; but this is only in God.

Secondly, grace is taken for the gifts of grace, and they are of two sorts, common or special.

Some are common to believers and hypocrites, such as a gift of knowledge, a gift of prayer, etc.#footnote[Gal 5:22, 23.]

Some are special graces, and they belong properly and only to the saints, such as faith, humility, meekness, love, patience, etc.

The second #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is wisely to consider the differences between renewing grace and#footnote[God does not bring a pair of scales to weigh our graces, but a touchstone to test our graces. Purity, preciousness, and holiness are stamped on all saving graces. Acts 15:9. 2 Pet 1:1. Jude 20.] restraining grace, between sanctifying grace and temporary grace. I will show you this in the following particulars:

1\. True grace makes all glorious within and without: “The king’s daughter is all glorious within; her gown is embroidered with gold.” True grace makes the understanding glorious, the will glorious, the affections glorious; it casts a general glory on all the noble parts of the soul: “The king’s daughter is all glorious within.” And as it makes the inside glorious, so it makes the outside glorious: “Her gown is embroidered with gold.” It makes men look gloriously and speak gloriously, and walk and act gloriously, so that worldly souls will be#footnote[Acts 4:8-15.] forced to say that these are the ones who have seen Jesus. As grace is a fire to burn up and consume the dross and filth of the soul, so it is an ornament to#footnote[2 Cor 5:17. Καινὴ κτίσις, a new creation: new Adam, new covenant, new paradise, new creation, new Lord, new law, new hearts, and new natures go together.] beautify and adorn the soul. True grace makes all new, the inside new and the outside new: “If anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation.” But temporary grace does not do this. True grace changes the very nature of a man; moral virtue only restrains or chains up the outward man, and does not change the whole man. A lion in a cage is a lion still: he is restrained, but not changed, for he keeps his lion-like nature still. So temporary graces restrain many men from this and that wickedness, but they do not change and turn their hearts from wickedness. But true grace turns a lion into a lamb, as you may see in Paul,#footnote[Acts 9.] and a notorious prostitute into a blessed and glorious penitent, as you may see in Mary Magdalene, etc.#footnote[Luke 7.]

2\. The objects of true grace are supernatural. True grace is occupied with the choicest and the highest objects,#footnote[2 Cor 4:18. Heb 11. Prov 15:24. A saint has his feet where other men’s heads are. Matt 6.] with the most soul-ennobling and soul-enlarging objects: God, Christ, precious promises that are worth more than a world, a kingdom that cannot be shaken, a crown of glory that does not wither, and heavenly treasures that do not rust. The objects of temporary grace are low and poor, and always within the compass of reason’s reach.

3\. True grace enables a Christian (when he is himself) to do spiritual actions with real pleasure and delight. To souls truly gracious, Christ’s “yoke is#footnote[Matt 11:30. 1 John 5:3. Rom 7:22.] easy,” and his “burden is light”; his “commandments are not burdensome,” but joyous. “In my inner being I delight in God’s law,” says Paul. The blessed man is described by this, that “his delight is in the Law#footnote[Ps 1:2.] of the #smallcaps[Lord].” “It is a joy to the righteous to do justice,”#footnote[Prov 21:15.] says Solomon. To a gracious soul all the ways of the Lord are pleasant, and all his paths are peace. But to souls that have only temporary grace, only moral virtues, religious services are a toil, not a pleasure; a burden, and not a delight. “Why have we fasted,” they say,#footnote[Isa 58:3.] “and You have not seen? Why have we humbled#footnote[Mal 3:14.] ourselves, and You have not noticed?” etc. “You#super[+] have said” (say those in Malachi), “‘It is futile to serve God. What have we gained by keeping His requirements and walking mournfully before the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts?’” “When will the New Moon be over” (say those in Amos), “that#footnote[Amos 8:5.] we may sell grain? When will the Sabbath end, that we may market wheat? Let us reduce the ephah and increase the shekel; let us cheat with dishonest scales.”

Fourthly, true grace makes a man most careful and most fearful about his own heart. It makes him most studious of his own heart, instructing it, examining it, and#footnote[Ps 51:10. Ps 119:36, 80. Ps 139:23. Ps 86:11. Matt 23.] watching over it. But temporary grace, moral virtues, make men more mindful and careful of others, to instruct them, counsel them, stir them up, and watch over them, etc. This demonstrates with open mouth that their graces are not saving graces belonging only to saints, but that they are temporary, and no more than what Judas, Demas, and the Pharisees had, etc.

5\. Grace will work a man’s heart to love and cling to the strictest and holiest ways and things of God, for their purity and holiness, in the face of all dangers and hardships: “Your word is completely#footnote[Ps 119:140.] pure; therefore Your servant loves it.” Others love it and like it and follow it for the credit, the honor, the advantage that they get by it; but I love it for its spiritual beauty and purity. So the psalmist: “All this has come upon us,#footnote[Ps 44:17, 18, 19. Grace is a full suit of armor against all troubles, and a paradise of all pleasures.] though we have not forgotten You or betrayed Your covenant. Our hearts have not turned back; our steps have not strayed from Your path, though You have crushed us in the lair of jackals and covered us with deepest darkness.” But temporary grace will not bear up the soul against all oppositions and discouragements in the ways of God, as is clear from their falling away in John 6#footnote[Matt 13:20, 21.] and from the stony ground’s falling away, etc.

6\. True grace will enable a man to step over the world’s crown to take up Christ’s cross, to prefer the cross of Christ above the glory and splendor of this world. It enabled Abraham and Moses and Daniel, with those other#footnote[Heb 11.] worthies in Hebrews 11, to do so.

Godfrey of Bouillon, first king of Jerusalem, refused to be crowned with a crown of gold, saying that it was#footnote[Few are of Jerome’s mind, who would rather have St. Paul’s coat with his heavenly graces than the purple of kings with their kingdoms. 2 Tim 4:10. Matt 19:20, 21, 22. The king of Navarre told Beza that in the cause of religion he would launch no further into the sea than he could be sure of returning safe to the harbor.] not fitting for a Christian to wear a crown of gold where Christ had worn a crown of thorns. Oh, but temporary grace cannot work the soul to prefer Christ’s cross above the world’s crown. When these two meet, a temporary Christian steps over Christ’s cross to take up and keep up the world’s crown: “Demas, in his love of this world, has deserted me.” So the young man in the gospel had many good things in him; he seemed well set for heaven, and came near to heaven. But when Christ set his cross before him, he stepped over that to enjoy the world’s crown. When Christ told him to “go, sell your possessions and give to the poor,” etc., “he went away in sorrow, because he had great wealth.” If heaven is to be had on no other terms, Christ may keep his heaven to himself; he will have none of it, etc.

Then, in the seventh place, consider this: sanctifying grace, renewing grace, sets the soul on spiritual duties from spiritual and inward motives, such as the sense of divine love, which constrains the soul to wait on#footnote[“As what I have, if offered to you without myself, does not please you, O Lord, so the good things we have from you, though they may refresh us, yet do not satisfy us without yourself.” Bernard. It is an excellent saying of Bernard (#emph[bonus es domine, animae quaerenti; quid invenienti?]): “You are good, O Lord, to the soul that seeks you; what are you then to the soul that finds you?”] God and to act for God; and the sense of the excellency and sweetness of communion with God; and the choice and precious revelations that the soul has had in the past of the beauty and glory of God, while it was in the service of God. The kind looks, the kind words, the blessed love letters, the glorious kisses, and the sweet embraces that gracious souls have had from Christ in his service stir them up and move them to wait on him in holy duties. Ah, but restraining grace, temporary grace, sets men on religious duties only from outward motives: the ear of men, the eye of men, the rewards of men, the keeping up of a name among men, and a thousand such considerations, as you may see in Saul, Jehu, Judas, Demas, and the scribes and Pharisees, etc.

The abbot in Melanchthon’s story lived strictly, walked demurely, and looked humble, as long as he was only a monk. But when, by his seeming extraordinary holiness, he became abbot, he grew intolerably proud and insolent. When he was asked the reason for it, he confessed that his former lowly look was only to see if he could find the keys of the abbey. Such poor, low, vain motives move temporary souls to all the service they perform, etc.

8\. Saving grace, renewing grace, will cause a man to follow the Lord fully, in forsaking all sin and in keeping all God’s precepts. Joshua and#footnote[Num 14:24. וימלא, has fully followed me: a metaphor taken from a ship under sail that is strongly carried along by the wind, fearing neither rocks nor sands. Luke 1:5, 6. Rev 14:4. Matt 23:23.] Caleb followed the Lord fully. Zechariah and Elizabeth were righteous before God, walking blamelessly in all the commandments and ordinances of the Lord. The saints in Revelation are described by this, that “they follow the Lamb wherever He goes.” But restraining grace, temporary grace, cannot enable a man to follow the Lord fully. All that temporary grace can enable a man to do is to follow the Lord partially, unevenly, and haltingly, as you may see in Jehu, Herod, Judas, and the scribes and Pharisees, who paid tithes of mint and anise and cumin, but neglected the weightier matters of the law: justice, mercy, and faith, etc.

True grace works the heart to the#footnote[Ps 119:104, 128. “I would rather go to hell pure from sin than to heaven polluted with that filth,” says Anselm.] hatred of all sin and to the love of all truth. It works a man to hate those sins that for the life of him he cannot conquer, and to loathe those sins that he would give all the world to overcome. So a soul truly gracious can say, “Though no one sin is mortified and subdued in me as it should be and as I would have it, yet every sin is hated and loathed by me.” So a soul truly gracious can say, “Though I do not obey any one command as I should and as I would, yet every word is sweet, every command of God is precious. I dearly prize and greatly love those#footnote[#emph[Da quod jubes & jube quod vis,] give what you command, and command what you will. Ps 119:119, 127, 167.] commands that I cannot obey. Though there are many commands that I cannot fulfill in a strict sense, yet there is no command that I would not fulfill, and none that I do not exceedingly love.” “I love Your commandments more than gold, even the purest gold.” “I obey Your testimonies and love them exceedingly.”

9\. True grace leads the soul to rest in Christ as in its chief good (#emph[summum bonum]); it works the soul to center in Christ as in its highest and ultimate end. “To whom would we go? You have#footnote[John 6:68. Song 5:10. Song 3:4. Grace is that star that leads to Christ; it is that cloud and pillar of fire that leads the soul to that heavenly Canaan where Christ sits chief.] the words of eternal life.” “My beloved is dazzling and ruddy, outstanding among ten thousand.” “I found the one my soul loves. I held him and would not let go.”

That wisdom a believer has from Christ leads him to center in the wisdom of Christ; that love the soul has from Christ leads the soul to center in the love of Christ; and that righteousness the soul has from Christ leads the soul to rest and center in the righteousness of Christ.#footnote[1 Cor 1:30. Phil 3:9.] True grace is a beam of Christ, and where it is, it will naturally lead the soul to rest in Christ. The stream does not lead more naturally to the fountain, nor the effect to the cause, than true grace leads the soul to Christ. But restraining grace, temporary grace, works the soul to center and rest in things below Christ. At times it works the soul to center in the praises of men, at times to rest in#footnote[Matt 6:1, 2. Zech 7:5, 6.] the rewards of men. “Truly I tell you, they already have their full reward,” says Christ; and so in a hundred other things, etc.

10\. True grace will enable a soul to sit down satisfied and content with the bare enjoyment of Christ. The#footnote[#emph[Cui cum paupertate bene convenit, pauper non est,] says Seneca. A contented man cannot be a poor man.] enjoyment of Christ without honor will satisfy the soul; the enjoyment of Christ without riches, the enjoyment of Christ without pleasures and without the smiles of men, will content and satisfy the soul. “It is enough; Joseph is alive.” So says a gracious soul: “Though honor is not, and riches are not, and health is not, and#footnote[Charlemagne’s motto was #emph[Christus regnat, vincit, triumphat.] And so it is the saints’.] friends are not, etc., it is enough that Christ is, that he reigns, conquers, and triumphs.” Christ is the jar of manna, the jug of oil, a bottomless ocean of all comfort, contentment, and satisfaction. He who has him lacks nothing; he who lacks him enjoys nothing. “Having nothing” (says Paul), “and yet possessing#footnote[2 Cor 6:10. St. Augustine, on Ps 12, brings in God rebuking a discontented Christian thus: “What is your faith? Have I promised you these things? What, were you made a Christian so that you should flourish here in this world?”] all things.” Oh, but a man who has only temporary grace, who has only restraining grace, cannot sit down satisfied and content when he lacks outward comforts. “Christ is good with honors,” says such a soul, “and Christ is good with riches, and Christ is good with pleasures, and he is good with such and such outward comforts. I must have Christ and the world, or else, like the young man in the gospel (in spite of my soul), I will forsake Christ to follow the world.” Ah, how many shining professing Christians there are in the world who cannot sit down satisfied and content when they lack this or that outward comfort and contentment, but are like the madmen of Bedlam, fretting and vexing, raging and raving, as if there were no God, no heaven, no hell, no Christ to make up all such outward lacks to souls! But a soul truly gracious can#footnote[Contentment is the deputy of outward happiness, and supplies its place where it is absent. As the Jews throw the book of Esther to the ground before they read it, because the name of God is not in it, as the rabbis have observed, so in some sense do saints with those mercies in which they do not read Christ’s name and see Christ’s heart. Luther said he would rather be in hell with Christ than in heaven without him.] say, “In having nothing, I have all things, because I have Christ. Having therefore all things in him, I seek no other reward, for he is the universal reward.” Such a soul can say, “Nothing is sweet to me without the enjoyment of Christ in it. Neither honors nor riches nor the smiles of men are sweet to me any further than I see Christ and taste Christ in them. The confluence of all outward good cannot make a heaven of glory in my soul if Christ, who is the height of my glory, is absent.” As Absalom said, “What is all this to me, as long as I cannot see the king’s face?” So says the soul: “Why do you tell me of this and that outward comfort, when I cannot see his face whom my soul loves? Why, my honor is not my Christ, nor are riches Christ, nor is the favor of men Christ. Let me have him, and let the men of this world take the world and divide it among themselves. I prize my Christ above all; I would enjoy my Christ before all other things in the world. His presence will make up for the absence of all other comforts, and his absence will darken and embitter all my comforts, so that my comforts will neither taste like comforts, nor look like comforts, nor warm like comforts, etc., when he who should#footnote[Lam 1:16.] comfort my soul stands far off,” etc. Christ is all, and in all, to souls truly gracious. We have all things in Christ, and Christ is all things to a Christian.#footnote[Col 3:11. “None but Christ, none but Christ,” said Lambert, lifting up his hands, the ends of his fingers flaming.] If we are sick, he is a physician; if we thirst, he is a fountain; if our sins trouble us, he is righteousness; if we need help, he is mighty to save; if we fear death, he is life; if we are in darkness, he is light; if we are weak, he is strength; if we are in poverty, he is plenty; if we desire heaven, he is the way. The soul cannot say, “This I would have, and that I would have,” but Christ says, “It is in me, it is in me, eminently, perfectly, eternally.”
